    <h2>The Climate Is the Whole Story</h2>

    <p>Hartselle sits in a warm, humid climate that mildew, algae, and mold love. Any surface that holds moisture — north-facing walls that never get direct sun, concrete under a tree, the shaded side of a brick chimney, siding near the damp ground — becomes a place for that growth to take hold and spread. It's why a house that got cleaned last year looks dingy again this year, and why the homes near shaded lots and the low, damp spots around the county grow it fastest. This isn't a dirt problem you rinse away; it's a biological one you have to treat, and the climate guarantees it keeps coming.</p>

    <h2>Why High Pressure Is the Wrong Tool</h2>

    <p>The instinct is more pressure — hit it harder and it'll come off. The result is worse than it sounds. High pressure, in the 2,500-to-3,000-psi range, does two bad things at once: it forces water behind vinyl siding where it can't dry, eats the mortar out of soft older brick, furs and gouges wood, and strips granules off a shingle roof — while only removing the surface layer of growth and leaving the living organism to bloom back within weeks. You get damage and a short-lived result from the same swing. The practitioner truth: the force was never what cleaned it best; the right detergent was.</p>

    <h2>What Actually Works Here: Soft-Washing</h2>

    <p>Soft-washing is the method that fits this climate. It uses low pressure — under about 500 psi, closer to a garden hose than a jet — paired with a cleaning solution that kills the mildew and algae at the root rather than just knocking the top off. Because it destroys the organism instead of relocating it, the surface stays clean for a full season instead of a few damp weeks, and because it's gentle, it's safe on the vinyl, brick, wood, and shingles that high pressure wrecks. Roofs in particular should only ever be soft-washed. High pressure still has a place — bare concrete driveways and flatwork can take it — which is why a good operator matches the method to the surface instead of using one setting on everything. Pressure washing pairs naturally with the exterior trades: it's the wash step before a <a href="/locations/hartselle/painter/">painter</a> can prep a surface, and it clears the oxidation and grime off a <a href="/locations/hartselle/gutter-services/">gutter</a> face without denting it.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Pressure Washing Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Real ranges:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>House soft-wash</strong> — about $250 to $500 for a typical single-story, more with heavy growth or two stories.</li>
      <li><strong>Driveway or patio</strong> — roughly $150 to $300.</li>
      <li><strong>Roof soft-wash</strong> — priced higher for access and the care it requires.</li>
      <li><strong>Annual package</strong> — the honest way to buy it here, since the growth returns every year.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The honest admission most companies won't lead with: in this climate a one-time wash is not permanent, because the mildew comes back with the next humid season. Annual service isn't a sales trick — it's what the climate actually requires.</p>

    <h2>The Right Time to Wash</h2>

    <p>Late spring is the natural time — clearing the winter's growth before the humid months and getting the house camera-ready for the season. If you're painting, wash first and let it dry; if you're selling, a clean exterior is one of the cheapest ways to lift curb appeal before photos. And if you've watched growth creep up the north wall for two years running, that's the sign to put it on an annual schedule rather than fighting it one panicked cleaning at a time.</p>

    <h2>Why It's a Yearly Relationship Here</h2>

    <p>Because the growth is seasonal and the surfaces vary, pressure washing is one of the few exterior trades that's genuinely a yearly relationship rather than a one-off. The <a href="/locations/hartselle/">Hartselle contractor overview</a> shows how it fits alongside the other exterior trades a homeowner cycles through, and the operator who explains the annual reality honestly is the one who earns the standing appointment.</p>

    <h2>Pressure Washing Questions We Hear a Lot</h2>

    <p><strong>Why does the green film keep coming back?</strong></p>
    <p>It's living mildew and algae fed by the humidity, not dirt. High pressure removes the surface but leaves the organism to regrow. A soft-wash detergent kills it so it stays gone longer.</p>

    <p><strong>Will pressure washing hurt my siding or roof?</strong></p>
    <p>High pressure can — water behind vinyl, eaten mortar, stripped shingles. Siding and roofs should be soft-washed under about 500 psi.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>House wash $250 to $500, driveway $150 to $300, and it's honestly an annual service in this climate.</p>

